          "The physical and regulatory importation of commercial cargo into India is governed by an exacting procedural sequence codified in Chapter VI and Chapter VII of the Customs Act, 1962. The process initiates when a conveyance carrying imported cargo approaches an Indian customs port or airport. Under Section 29, the master of a vessel or pilot of an aircraft shall not permit the conveyance to call or land at any place other than an officially notified customs port or customs airport. Under Section 30, the person-in-charge of the conveyance is statutorily mandated to deliver to the proper officer an Arrival Manifest or Electronic Cargo Manifest (ECM) prior to the arrival of the vessel or aircraft. The manifest contains an exhaustive inventory of all cargo, container numbers, consignee details, and packages carried on board. Failure to present the cargo manifest within the statutory timeline attracts penal liabilities under Section 117.",
