          "The statutory mechanics governing the outbound movement of goods from India are codified in Chapter VII and Chapter VIII of the Customs Act, 1962. Under Section 2(18), export with its grammatical variations and cognate expressions means taking out of India to a place outside India. The legal process begins with the exporter obtaining an Importer Exporter Code (IEC) issued by the Directorate General of Foreign Trade (DGFT) under Section 7 of the Foreign Trade (Development and Regulation) Act, 1992. No person can export goods without an active IEC linked to their GSTIN and bank account on the ICEGATE electronic portal. The exporter must file an electronic Shipping Bill or Bill of Export under Section 50 of the Customs Act prior to the loading of goods onto the export conveyance.",
          "Under Section 50, the exporter must categorize the export transaction by selecting the appropriate Shipping Bill format based on export incentives and duty liabilities. The primary statutory classifications include: the Dutiable Shipping Bill (printed on yellow stationery in physical eras, now designated by code 10 on ICEGATE) for commodities subjected to export duty under the Second Schedule to the Customs Tariff Act, such as iron ore, high-grade bauxite, and specific agricultural commodities; the Duty Drawback Shipping Bill (Code 20) for exports claiming refund of customs duties under Section 74 or Section 75; the Free Shipping Bill (Code 40) for goods not subject to export duty and not claiming drawback; and the Advance Authorization or EPCG Shipping Bill (Code 30) for goods exported in discharge of export obligations under concessional import licenses.",
          "Simultaneously, the carrier of the outbound conveyance is governed by rigorous statutory constraints under maritime customs law. Under Section 39 of the Customs Act, the master of a vessel shall not permit the loading of any export goods until the proper customs officer has granted Entry Outwards for that vessel. Entry Outwards signifies that the vessel has completed its inbound customs discharge obligations, has undergone port health clearance, and is legally authorized to embark cargo destined for foreign ports. Furthermore, under Section 40, the master shall not permit any export goods to be loaded unless the exporter presents a valid Shipping Bill that has been duly processed and endorsed by the proper customs officer. The physical presentation and automated verification of cargo at the customs terminal involve non-intrusive container scanning and risk management evaluation.",
