          "The statutory classification of goods under Indian customs law is governed by the Customs Tariff Act, 1975, which incorporates the international Harmonized Commodity Description and Coding System, commonly known as the Harmonized System Nomenclature (HSN). Developed under the auspices of the World Customs Organization (WCO) through the International Convention on the Harmonized Commodity Description and Coding System signed in Brussels in 1983, the HSN provides a standardized, multi-purpose goods nomenclature utilized by over two hundred nations and customs unions globally. The First Schedule to the Customs Tariff Act, 1975, establishes the Import Tariff, organized systematically into twenty-one Sections and ninety-eight Chapters. Chapter 77 is intentionally reserved by the WCO for future international allocation, while Chapter 98 is reserved specifically under Indian national law to accommodate Project Imports, Laboratory Chemicals, and Passenger Baggage.",
          "The coding architecture of the Indian Customs Tariff operates on a rigorous eight-digit hierarchy. The initial two digits identify the Chapter within the relevant Section (for example, Chapter 84 covering nuclear reactors, boilers, machinery, and mechanical appliances). The third and fourth digits designate the Heading within that Chapter (such as Heading 8471 covering automatic data processing machines and units thereof). The fifth and sixth digits represent the Subheading, which constitutes the internationally harmonized standard standardized by the WCO across all contracting parties. Up to the six-digit subheading level, no member nation may unilaterally alter the description or scope of a commodity. The seventh and eighth digits constitute the Tariff Item, representing national statutory subdivisions enacted by the Indian Parliament to levy differentiated rates of Basic Customs Duty, impose policy restrictions under the Foreign Trade Policy, or monitor strategic import volumes.",
          "A cardinal principle of tariff classification is that the statutory text of Section Notes and Chapter Notes possesses statutory force equal to the enactments of Parliament itself. In the landmark Supreme Court decision of Collector of Central Excise v. Wood Craft Products Ltd. (1995), the apex court held that Section Notes and Chapter Notes are statutory definitions enacted by the legislature, and where a specific definition or exclusion is provided in a Chapter Note, it overrides any competing classification derived from popular trade meaning. Consequently, when determining the classification of an imported article, a tax professional must first examine whether the article is expressly included or excluded by the relevant Chapter Note, before embarking on general semantic analyses.",
          "A vital national statutory feature within the First Schedule is Heading 9801, which governs Project Imports under the Project Import Regulations, 1986. Under Heading 9801, an enterprise establishing an industrial plant, power generation project, irrigation facility, or mining development can import diverse machinery, electrical equipment, prime movers, instruments, and raw materials under a single, unified tariff heading with a concessional, consolidated rate of Basic Customs Duty. This statutory mechanism eliminates the enormous administrative burden of classifying thousands of individual spare parts, valves, motors, and structural components across different chapters of the tariff. To claim this statutory benefit, the enterprise must formally register its supply contract with the jurisdictional customs house prior to the clearance of the first import consignment, submit detailed plant engineering layouts, and execute an undertaking to prove final commissioning within statutory deadlines.",
          "Where the statutory notes do not resolve the classification, Indian jurisprudence applies the well-established Trade Parlance Theory. In Dunlop India Ltd. v. Union of India (1976) and subsequently in Commissioner of Central Excise v. Connaught Plaza Restaurant (2012), the Supreme Court established that in the absence of a statutory definition in the Tariff Act, commodities must be classified according to the commercial understanding and meaning attributed to them by people who trade, buy, and sell those goods in ordinary business parlance, rather than scientific or dictionary definitions. However, trade parlance cannot be invoked to subvert or contradict the express language of the tariff heading or the binding definitions set forth in statutory Chapter Notes, preserving the integrity of the WCO coding architecture."
