          "The statutory valuation of imported goods for the assessment of ad valorem customs duties is governed by Section 14 of the Customs Act, 1962, read in conjunction with the Customs Valuation (Determination of Value of Imported Goods) Rules, 2007 (CVR, 2007). Under Section 14(1), the value of imported goods shall be the transaction value of such goods, that is to say, the price actually paid or payable for the goods when sold for export to India for delivery at the time and place of importation, where the buyer and seller of the goods are not related and price is the sole consideration for the sale. The transaction value represents the primary legal benchmark, reflecting India's adherence to the World Trade Organization (WTO) Customs Valuation Agreement (GATT Article VII). However, acceptance of the commercial invoice price as transaction value is conditioned upon the satisfaction of specific statutory criteria under Rule 3(2) of the CVR, 2007.",
          "Under Rule 3(2), the transaction value shall be accepted provided there are no restrictions on the disposition or use of the goods by the buyer (other than statutory restrictions), the sale is not subject to any condition or consideration for which a value cannot be determined, no part of the proceeds of any subsequent resale accrues to the seller without adjustment, and the buyer and seller are not related within the meaning of Rule 2(2). Where the parties are related\u2014such as in transactions between a foreign parent and an Indian subsidiary\u2014the transaction value may still be accepted under Rule 3(3) if the importer demonstrates that the relationship did not influence the price, or that the transaction value closely approximates a test value of identical or similar goods sold to unrelated buyers.",
          "Related party importations are subjected to specialized scrutiny by the Special Valuation Branch (SVB) under CBIC Circular No. 5/2016-Customs. Under modern SVB procedure, the historical practice of levying an Extra Duty Deposit (EDD) of one to five percent on all related party imports was abolished to facilitate ease of doing business. Importers submit detailed documentation under Annexure A and Annexure B questionnaires, providing transfer pricing study reports, audited financial statements, royalty agreements, and functional asset risk (FAR) analyses. The SVB issues an Investigation Report (IR) examining whether the intra-group transfer price includes concealed profit shifts or satisfies the arm's-length criteria under customs valuation jurisprudence.",
          "To arrive at the statutory Assessable Value under Section 14, the invoice price must be adjusted through the mandatory additions prescribed in Rule 10 of the CVR, 2007. Under Rule 10(1), there shall be added to the price actually paid or payable: (a) commissions and brokerage, except buying commissions; (b) the cost of containers and packing; (c) the apportioned value of goods and services supplied directly or indirectly by the buyer free of charge or at reduced cost for use in production (known as assists), such as tools, dies, moulds, and engineering, development, artwork, and design work undertaken elsewhere than in India; (d) royalties and license fees related to the imported goods that the buyer must pay as a condition of sale; and (e) the value of any part of the proceeds of any subsequent resale that accrues to the seller.",
