          "The Export Promotion Capital Goods (EPCG) Scheme, codified in Chapter 5 of the Foreign Trade Policy, is designed to facilitate the technological modernization of India's production base by enabling domestic manufacturers and service providers to import cutting-edge capital equipment at zero customs duty. Under Paragraph 5.01 of the FTP 2023, the Zero Duty EPCG Scheme permits the import of capital goods for pre-production, production, and post-production phases at zero Basic Customs Duty, zero Social Welfare Surcharge, and zero Integrated GST. Eligible capital equipment encompasses plant machinery, industrial tooling, computer hardware and software systems, testing jigs, dies, moulds, and spare parts. Second-hand capital goods, however, are strictly prohibited from being imported under the zero-duty EPCG framework.",
          "The statutory consideration demanded by the State in exchange for waiving customs duties on capital goods is the fulfillment of a dual Export Obligation (EO): a Specific Export Obligation and an Average Export Obligation. The Specific Export Obligation is codified as a mathematical multiple: the authorization holder must achieve an export performance equivalent to exactly six times the duties, taxes, and cess saved on the imported capital goods, within a statutory period of six years from the date of issue of the EPCG authorization. The duty saved is calculated on the CIF value of the imported machinery, aggregating the BCD, SWS, and IGST that would have been payable in the absence of the authorization.",
